<p>
Bozhidar Batsov has <a href="https://batsov.com/articles/2026/02/14/neocaml-0-1-ready-for-action/">announced the release of the first version of Neocaml</a>, an Emacs major mode for OCaml. Batsov is a big fan of OCaml but wasn&rsquo;t happy with the existing Emacs major modes for it. So, of course, <a href="https://batsov.com/articles/2025/03/14/neocaml-a-new-emacs-package-for-ocaml-programming/">he started working on his own version</a>. He&rsquo;s now reached the point where he feels it&rsquo;s ready to be shared so he&rsquo;s released <a href="https://github.com/bbatsov/neocaml">Version 0.1</a>. It&rsquo;s already on <a href="https://melpa.org/#/neocaml">MELPA</a> so it&rsquo;s easy to install if you want to try it out.
</p>
<p>
Batsov&rsquo;s post lays out what he feels was wrong with the existing implementations. You can read the details in his post but the TL;DR is that they&rsquo;re either old and not well maintained or they use the old style font-locking and indentation mechanisms that have been replaced by <a href="https://emacs-tree-sitter.github.io/">TreeSitter</a>.
</p>
<p>
Batsov says that <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/tuareg">tuareg</a> still has more features than Neocaml but that Neocaml has all the features you&rsquo;re apt to need. Batsov is, of course, still working on adding additional features including support for more file types and improvements to structured navigation using TreeSitter. He is, of course, very interested in bug reports and pull requests.</p>
